    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d950">

                <pb facs="0745" n="663" xml:id="pg663"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 817.00/1597, 1599.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The American Minister to
                        the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegrams—Paraphrase.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Managua</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1911-06-02"><hi rendition="#italic">June 2,
                        1911</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>The governor is instructed to exercise very great vigilance in Blue-fields and
                    the Bluff. The Government learns that the filibuster <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Eureka</hi> went to Amapala where, on examination, nothing suspicious was
                    found; the Government believes arms were landed on some island in the Gulf, and
                    greatly desires that the Government of Salvador make a search and secure them
                    before removed by the revolutionists.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Northcott.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
